### Page 19
**2. Algorithm for Spinal Cord Injury**
*(Image translation)*
**ACUTE SPINAL CORD INJURY & SHOCK DIFFERENTIATION**
*Cervical Spine Trauma + Neurological Deficit*

*   **NEUROGENIC SHOCK (HEMODYNAMIC)**
    *   Mechanism: Loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone (T6 or higher)
    *   Triad: Hypotension + Bradycardia + Warm/Dry Skin
    *   Treatment: IV Crystalloid Challenge + Norepinephrine (First-Line)
    *   Steroids (Methylprednisolone): STRICTLY NOT RECOMMENDED
*   **SPINAL SHOCK (NEUROLOGICAL)**
    *   Mechanism: Complete transient loss of all spinal cord reflexes
    *   Signs: Flaccid paralysis + Areflexia + Absent sphincter tone
    *   Resolution Marker: Return of Bulbocavernosus Reflex
    *   Distinguish from Hypovolemic Shock: Rule out internal bleeding FIRST!

**INCOMPLETE SPINAL CORD SYNDROMES**
*   **Central Cord Syndrome:** Weakness Upper Extremities > Lower Extremities (hyperextension in elderly)
*   **Anterior Cord Syndrome:** Bilateral motor paralysis + loss of pain/temp; PRESERVED vibration/proprioception
*   **Brown-Sequard Syndrome:** Ipsilateral motor paralysis & proprioception loss + Contralateral pain/temp loss
*   **Imaging:** Emergent Non-Contrast CT Occiput-to-T1 + MRI for ligamentous / cord injury
